---
import CalculatorLayout from '@/layouts/CalculatorLayout.astro';
import SimpleInterestCalculator from '@/components/calculators/SimpleInterestCalculator';

const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'What is the simple interest formula?',
    a: 'I = P × r × t: interest equals the principal, times the yearly rate as a decimal, times the time in years. $10,000 at 5% for 3 years earns 10,000 × 0.05 × 3 = $1,500.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What is the difference between simple and compound interest?',
    a: 'Simple interest is only ever charged or earned on the original principal. Compound interest is also earned on past interest. Over 10 years at 5%, $10,000 grows to $15,000 with simple interest but $16,288.95 with yearly compounding.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How do I calculate simple interest for days or months?',
    a: 'Convert the time to years first: divide months by 12 or days by 365. For $20,000 at 8% for 90 days: 20,000 × 0.08 × 90 ÷ 365 = $394.52.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Where is simple interest used?',
    a: 'Short-term loans, some car loans, certain bonds, treasury bills, personal loans between friends and many interest calculations for late payments. Most savings accounts and mortgages use compound interest instead.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How do I find the rate or time from the interest?',
    a: 'Rearrange the formula: rate = I ÷ (P × t), and time = I ÷ (P × r). Choose Rate or Time at the top of the calculator and it does this for you.',
  },
];
---

<CalculatorLayout
  slug="simple-interest-calculator"
  title="Simple Interest Calculator – I = P × r × t"
  description="Free simple interest calculator. Work out interest, principal, rate or time with I = P × r × t, for days, months or years, and compare with compound interest."
  intro="Work out simple interest, or solve for the principal, rate or time, in days, months or years, and see how it compares with compound interest."
  faqs={faqs}
  updated="2026-09-27"
>
  <SimpleInterestCalculator client:load />

  <Fragment slot="learn">
    <h2>How to use the simple interest calculator</h2>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Choose what to work out:</strong> interest, principal, rate or time.</li>
      <li><strong>Enter the other three values.</strong> Time can be in years, months or days.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Simple interest formulas</h2>
    <div class="formula">I = P × r × t &nbsp;&nbsp; P = I ÷ (r × t) &nbsp;&nbsp; r = I ÷ (P × t) &nbsp;&nbsp; t = I ÷ (P × r)</div>
    <p>Here r is the yearly rate as a decimal (5% = 0.05) and t is in years. The total amount at the end is P + I.</p>

    <h3>Worked examples</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Interest:</strong> $10,000 at 5% for 3 years: 10,000 × 0.05 × 3 = <strong>$1,500</strong>, so you have $11,500.</li>
      <li><strong>Time:</strong> how long does $10,000 at 6% take to earn $1,500? 1,500 ÷ (10,000 × 0.06) = <strong>2.5 years</strong>.</li>
      <li><strong>Days:</strong> $20,000 at 8% for 90 days: 20,000 × 0.08 × 90 ÷ 365 = <strong>$394.52</strong>.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Simple vs compound interest</h2>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>$10,000 at 5%</th><th>Simple</th><th>Compound (yearly)</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>After 3 years</td><td>$11,500</td><td>$11,576.25</td></tr>
        <tr><td>After 5 years</td><td>$12,500</td><td>$12,762.82</td></tr>
        <tr><td>After 10 years</td><td>$15,000</td><td>$16,288.95</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>The gap grows over time because compound interest earns interest on interest. For long-term savings, use the <a href="/compound-interest-calculator">compound interest calculator</a>.</p>
  </Fragment>
</CalculatorLayout>
